<h2>Just Make Things</h2>
<p>
    Much of this document envisions an ideal piece of software.
    It is important to keep in mind that in many cases, perfectly-designed software is suboptimal.
    Software development is fundamentally about <em>making programs that do something</em>,
    and spending time writing perfect code is only worthwhile if it helps us make more software that works!
    It is not worth it to worry about writing more descriptive variable names in a short one-off script.
    It is not worth it to split code into many small modules and add a complex build process in a quick prototype.
    Only once the software has reached a stage of widespread use and complexity does it make sense to try to follow all best practices to the letter.
    Before then, you don't even know the software will be useful. You don't even know the problem yet,
    so why bother with clean code? Clean code isn't the goal, working software is.
    Things like refactoring an older codebase written when object-oriented programming was in style
    to make it more functional now that functional programming is in style,
    or migrating to the newest library
</p>

<h3>Modern</h3>
<p>
    The term "modern" is often used as justification for why some new technology is better.
    What does "modern" mean? It is meaningless.
    "Modern" is the term people use when they're trying to say that whatever you're using is uncool now 
    and whatever they're making is the new cool thing you should be using instead.
    This is thoroughly unconvincing. Often there <em>are</em> legitimate reasons to switch to new tools,
    but those reasons had better be because the new tool solves a non-trivial problem, not just because the new tool is now.
</p>

<h3>Programmer Career Stages</h3>
<p>
    There are three phases of being a developer.
    A beginner programmer can barely figure out how to get things done, and ends up making things one way, the way they know how.
    An intermediate programmer has learned many ways to get things done, and spends most of their time trying to figure out which strategy to use. 
    They are not much more efficient than the beginner programmer.
    An expert programmer knows many ways to get things done, and is able to quickly identify the best strategy for a given problem.
    They are vastly more efficient than either the beginner or the expert.
</p>

