<h3>Functions</h3>
<p>
    A function's arguments should include everything that it needs to use.
Sometimes this is called "dependency injection", which is a needlessly confusing term.

Pure functions are nice, but sometimes it's easier and better to just have side effects.
It's counterproductive to bend over backwards and write confusing, slow code in places where
just modifying the input object would be simple enough. And sometimes side effects are unavoidable, anyway.
</p>




<h3>Object-Oriented Programming</h3>
<p>
    and memory management easier
    OOP is a strategy for making code easier to understand, at the cost of performance.
In domains where distinct "objects" are easy to recognize, such as game development, 
this can feel clean. In domains where the objects aren't so clear, it can actually make
the code harder to understand instead. OOP should therefore only be used when it is easy to
divide the code into objects. It should not be dogmatically applied to an entire codebase,
because then you end up with `Abstract` and confusion. 
Clean code is subjective, so OOP is not *inherently* better than other clean programming strategies.
It is only better when the developers working on the code think it's easier to understand.
But its performance costs are *not* subjective, so it should not be the default technique.

Don't like inheritance
Don't like polymorpishm
Do like encapsulation at module boundaries
</p>

