<h2>AI</h2>
<p>
    I started this document by stating that most of this only matters situuationally because the rules may be bent to get things done.
    Now I end this document by stating that none of this matters because of AI.

    For the past year I have done work in AI training, providing programming expertise for model data.
    As a result, I am somewhat in-tune with what the latest models can do, because I try to get them to fail.
    Well, if a programming tasks is well specified, they can get it done almost 100% of the time.
    It may not be the simplest representation, but it will get it done.

     * Developers are responsible for their LLM output - just like a boss is ultimately responsible for their team
    * Link to AI puzzle site
    * AI is fantastic at generalizations and terrible at specifics

    Right now, LLMs are very good at writing 90% of code. It would be foolish to avoid them entirely
when the goal is to get things done.

However, I *strongly* believe that we should understand what we are doing.
For that reason, I do not "vibe code". I only rarely use LLMs as agents, and I always read what they produce.
When I'm learning something new, I don't let AI write any of the code at all.

At some point in the future, AI will be better than all humans at writing code.
This makes me uncomfortable and sad. 
Until then, I have to believe that my ability to understand code 
and the core purpose of software at a deeper level than the LLMs is valuable.
</p>






